## 90-second walkthrough

> "Varaha has publicly said its Agnisparsh project plans about 450 Kon-Tiki kilns and more than 100,000 tonnes in 2027. I wanted to know what has to be true for that to work, so I built the control tower an ops team would use to run it, on synthetic data.
>
> **First, the arithmetic.** 100,000 over 450 kilns is about <<anchor_kiln>> net tonnes per kiln-year, roughly <<anchor_ramp>> once you allow for the ramp. On published Kon-Tiki performance, a typical kiln gets nowhere near that: the network would deliver about <<lit_net>> tonnes. So the plan has to rest on a design point I can name: <<plan_dry>>-tonne batches in about <<plan_kiln_m3>>-cubic-metre kilns, <<plan_bpd>> batches a day, and feedstock dried below <<plan_moisture>> moisture, which cuts the methane deduction from <<ch4_share_30>> to <<ch4_share_5>> of gross removals.
>
> **Second, uncertainty.** That design point gives <<plan_det>> tonnes if everything is nominal. Across <<mc_runs>> Monte Carlo runs the P50 is <<plan_p50>>, because most inputs sit at the good end of their range. The plan needs about <<contingency>> tonnes of contingency.
>
> **Third, where to buy it.** The lever simulator ranks options by rupees per incremental tonne. Fixing dMRV evidence capture recovers tonnes at about <<lev1_cost>> a tonne. Every capacity lever costs about <<lev2_cost>>, close to full unit cost. So fix MRV before adding kilns.
>
> **Finally, integrity.** In the farmer ledger, one site paid villages late. Four to six weeks later its supply roughly halved. Paying farmers on time is a delivery control, and it is also what buyers and rating agencies check.
