## Likely questions

**1. Why is your persistence factor 0.80?**
It's the IPCC 2019 Refinement default for medium-temperature pyrolysis. Kon-Tiki's main zone reaches 680–750 °C (Schmidt 2016), which would justify the high-temperature value of 0.89. I stayed conservative because distributed kilns have mixed temperature histories and Indian soils are warm, which shortens persistence in decay models. It is methodology-dependent, so it's the first thing I'd confirm with the carbon team. Moving to 0.89 adds about <<pers_uplift>> to net tonnes.

**2. What would you measure first on day 1?**
Dry feedstock per batch and batches per running kiln-day, from batch logs at 3–5 sites. They are the two longest amber bars in the tornado, and they separate the <<lit_net>>-tonne world from the 100k-tonne world. The scales and timestamps the dMRV system already captures give both, so it costs almost nothing.

**3. How would you cut ₹/t by 15%?**
Feedstock is <<feed_share>> of cost, so the lever is tonnes of CO₂ per kilogram of residue, not the residue price. Three moves: lift the pass rate about <<dmrv_step>> pp (the same residue earns more credits), keep moisture at or below <<plan_moisture>> (the CH₄ deduction falls from <<ch4_share_15>> to <<ch4_share_5>> of gross if drying is slipping), and confirm high-temperature persistence if the methodology allows it. Each spreads the same biomass spend over more tonnes. Cutting the biomass price would erode supply, as the ledger shows.

**4. Your P50 is below the target. Are you saying 100k is unrealistic?**
No. I'm saying the plan has a design point that works, and every input is set at its nominal value. The P50 tells you how much buffer to build: about <<contingency>> tonnes. The lever list prices that buffer, and the top three levers close <<lev_top3_gap>> of it. The real data might also put throughput higher than my ranges, which would close the gap on its own.

**5. Why trust synthetic data?**
I don't trust it for levels. I use it to prove the control tower would catch the things that matter: the new-operator evidence spike, MRV-weak sites and the payment-to-supply lag. Every generator parameter is in the yaml and tagged as a synthetic-data design choice. Swap in real parquet files with the same schema and every page runs on them.

**6. What is the biggest risk you haven't modelled?**
Correlated downside. My Monte Carlo draws inputs independently, but a bad monsoon hits residue supply, moisture and operating days together. A correlated run would widen the P10. The second is credit price: at <<price_low>> every tonne loses money, so price risk is as big as delivery risk.

**7. Why does a dMRV QA desk beat adding kilns?**
A rejected batch has already paid for residue, labour and water. Recovering it only costs evidence review, about <<lev1_cost>> a tonne. A new tonne from a new kiln carries the full variable cost, about <<lev2_cost>> a tonne. Rejections cost <<risk2_t>> tonnes in the synthetic year, and the top two reasons (<<rej1>>, <<rej2>>) are <<rej12_share>> of them. Both are hardware-and-checklist problems.

**8. How would this work in Côte d'Ivoire?**
The playbook has an annex, flagged as hypotheses. The feedstock changes (cocoa pod husk, cashew shells), payments move to mobile money, training goes into French and local languages, and the monsoon calendar becomes two rainy seasons. The control tower stays the same: the same KPIs with new thresholds.

